                            <a name="comment2332"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The Master Narrative
</h4>

<p>
A while back, in another Kruse-inspired discussion, this one about rethinking the traditional newspaper and its beat system, we all talked about what the perfect beat might be. Kruse asked, "What would you write about if all bets were off?" I said this:<br />
<br />
"Desperation. Or, the things people do when they are forced to do something (abandon morals, embrace new ones, kill others, kill themselves, learn things, beg, win, lose, ignore the problem, take risks, become the Wiggles)."<br />
<br />
This is relevant here because, since then, through sheer will and lots of bitching and begging, I wriggled out of my old, traditional beat and became a GA reporter. My task is to write those stories that will "get the readers talking," the editors say. I immediately set about on this new boundary-less gig intent on fulfilling the promise of what I'd earlier written here.<br />
<br />
It did not work, primarily because of a fatal flaw in the premise: Not all stories arise out of desperation.<br />
<br />
But behind every story, it seems to me, are choices. And so ... this: My beat, at least as defined in my head now, is Choices. I write whenever I can about the choices people make, the reasons for those choices, the consequences of those choices. It is an angle from which I approach stories now. Aside from breaking stuff or random GA assignments that are not really stories, it is an approach I try to use on nearly every story. The Katrina victims whose choices lead to them remaining in FEMA trailers two years later. The mayor who sweats over a jury's reaction to his questionable choice. The burn victim who is faced with the choice of resembling a monster or presenting a mask to the world.<br />
<br />
Now, looking back on this collection of stories about choices, I see hits and misses. I see woefully underreported stories, and those that were written poorly. But in this gallery of hits and misses, I also see something resembling RPC's master narrative. I can look at the collection as one big story, with each edition an anecdote of sorts. A mounting of the evidence. This big story is about what makes us human. It is about hope and risk, success and failure. It is my interpretation of the reality that is Mississippi in 2007, through the story of its people.<br />
<br />
So, does anybody else do this, or have I gone off the deep end?
</p>
